PHASE 2 — SNAP Measures
Measure 1 of 5: SNAP Rate (All Households)
SNAP Rate =
DIVIDE(
    CALCULATE(
        SUM(fact_acs_wi_county[estimate]),
        fact_acs_wi_county[var_base] = "B22002_002"
    ),
    CALCULATE(
        SUM(fact_acs_wi_county[estimate]),
        fact_acs_wi_county[var_base] = "B22002_001"
    ),
    0
)
Format: Percentage, 1 decimal place.

Measure 2 of 5: SNAP Count (All Households)
SNAP Count =
CALCULATE(
    SUM(fact_acs_wi_county[estimate]),
    fact_acs_wi_county[var_base] = "B22002_002"
)
Format: Whole number, comma separated (in Power BI: choose Whole Number format, turn on thousands separator).

Measure 3 of 5: Female Head of Household SNAP Count
This sums _006 (female HoH, with children, SNAP) and _012 (female HoH, no children, SNAP):
Female HoH SNAP Count =
CALCULATE(
    SUM(fact_acs_wi_county[estimate]),
    fact_acs_wi_county[var_base] IN {"B22002_006", "B22002_012"}
)
Format: Whole number, comma separated.

Measure 4 of 5: Female Head of Household SNAP Rate
Denominator is all female HoH households (SNAP + non-SNAP, with and without children):
Female HoH SNAP Rate =
DIVIDE(
    CALCULATE(
        SUM(fact_acs_wi_county[estimate]),
        fact_acs_wi_county[var_base] IN {"B22002_006", "B22002_012"}
    ),
    CALCULATE(
        SUM(fact_acs_wi_county[estimate]),
        fact_acs_wi_county[var_base] IN {
            "B22002_006", "B22002_012",
            "B22002_019", "B22002_025"
        }
    ),
    0
)
Format: Percentage, 1 decimal place.

Measure 5 of 5: Female HoH SNAP with Children Count
This isolates just households with children under 18:
Female HoH SNAP With Children =
CALCULATE(
    SUM(fact_acs_wi_county[estimate]),
